 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>For his fourth collection at Mithridate, Daniel Fletcher looked to the New Romantics and the distinctive universe they conjured from whatever they could get their hands on: the costume box of an abandoned theater ransacked for its tutus, a brother’s rugby jerseys liberated from a bedroom and styled for the club. This came to life on the first day of London Fashion Week in a cavernous basement configured as a nightclub—Angelo Badalamenti’s <em>Twin Peaks</em> instrumentals welcomed guests into the dark, sporadically spotlit space, before a David Bowie-led soundtrack led the runway.</p><p>“The last few seasons were really establishing what Mithridate is,” Fletcher said at a preview in the brand’s sun-striped London studio. “With this one, it’s like, Okay, so we know what it is now—how can we push that forward?” His answer was more playful and wickedly feminine. Military braiding migrated onto silk shirts and party dresses; gold logo buttons marched across henleys; strong shoulders met tiny knit short-shorts. Candy-pink tutus converged with more structured shirts and striped button-ups were contoured by bodysuits with garter hooks swinging like a metronome from their hems, while gauzy sheer knits provided a new kind of sensual strain. A whorling-skirted, shocking red “Divine dress” nodded to John Waters’s <em>Pink Flamingos</em>. Charm-adorned cummerbunds cinched draped gowns and pencil skirts, and corsets peeked from men’s impeccably tailored suit pants. Tricorne-style hats, created with his collaborator, the milliner Noel Stewart, were delightfully jaunty. “I’m always playing with taking menswear and making it into a womenswear wardrobe,” Fletcher said. “This is probably the clearest example of that so far, and vice-versa.”</p><p>Fletcher described the color palette affectionately as “sickly—colors that shouldn’t work together”: gray-violets, ochre against petrol blue; hot pink with scarlet; cornflower against crystalline green. This slightly wrong quality extended to the proportions, from supersized studded denim to extra-elongated bags, and to familiar pieces nudged into unexpected shapes and stature: cable knits topped with stiff bows, silk scarves worked around cheeky briefs, and a now-signature sporty flat was recast as a sexy kitten heel.</p><p>Four seasons in, Fletcher has a firmer sense of which codes are worth repeating. Covered and decorative buttons, integrated belts, and collegiate knitwear shapes recur; this season also brings the label’s first monogram. “I’m not about trying to recreate a British heritage brand,” Fletcher said. The question, rather, is what the Mithridate equivalent of those accumulated house codes might look like without contriving an archive. A monogram could become one such device: blown up and shrunk down, or continually reworked. These repetitions have not meant settling, especially on craft. An architecturally-hipped dress made of pointed feathers, and an undulating tartan impressively, painstakingly constructed from individually counted strands of beads demonstrated the growing fluency between Fletcher’s London team and Mithridate’s atelier in China. “We’ve really found our rhythm now,” he said.</p><p>That confidence may be why spring for Mithridate feels lighter on its feet. The New Romantics made their own realities of glamour, crafting something from nothing and dressing for themselves; Fletcher’s Mithridate, now, finds that the fun lies in seeing what he can do with the world he has built.</p></div>",
